13	    /// that allows users to navigate pages, in the same page, same website or on another website.
14	    ///
15	    /// While before it was possible to attach logic to an anchor element, with the advent of JSX libraries,
16	    /// it's now  easier to attach logic to any HTML element, anchors included.
17	    ///
18	    /// This rule is designed to prevent users from attaching logic at the click of anchors when the `href`
19	    /// provided to the anchor element is not valid. Avoid using `#` symbol inside the `href` when you are
20	    /// attaching the logic to the anchor element. If the anchor has logic attached to it with an incorrect `href`
21	    /// the rules suggests to turn it to a `button`, because that's likely what the user wants.
22	    ///
23	    /// Anchor `<a></a>` elements should be used for navigation, while `<button></button>` should be
24	    /// used for user interaction.
25	    ///
26	    /// There are **many reasons** why an anchor should not have a logic with an incorrect `href` attribute:
27	    /// - it can disrupt the correct flow of the user navigation e.g. a user that wants to open the link
28	    /// in another tab, but the default "click" behavior is prevented
29	    /// - it can source of invalid links, and crawlers can't navigate the website, risking to penalize
30	    /// SEO ranking
31	    ///
32	    ///
33	    /// For a detailed explanation, check out https://marcysutton.com/links-vs-buttons-in-modern-web-applications
34	    ///
35	    /// ## Examples
36	    ///
37	    /// ### Invalid
38	    ///
39	    /// ```jsx,expect_diagnostic
40	    /// <a href={null}>navigate here</a>
41	    /// ```
42	    /// ```jsx,expect_diagnostic
43	    /// <a href={undefined}>navigate here</a>
44	    /// ```
45	    /// ```jsx,expect_diagnostic
46	    /// <a href>navigate here</a>
47	    /// ```
48	    /// ```jsx,expect_diagnostic
